Lantern Press: Formatting and Style Guidelines

1. Manuscript Formatting
All submissions to Lantern Press must adhere to a strict set of formatting rules to ensure uniformity during the typesetting and production phases. Manuscripts should be formatted using standard 12-point Times New Roman font. The use of elaborate fonts or varied sizes within the main body text is strictly prohibited, as it disrupts the automated copy-editing software.

Margins must be set to exactly one inch on all sides (top, bottom, left, and right). The text should be aligned to the left, with a jagged right edge; never use full justification, as this creates uneven spacing that must be manually corrected by the production team.

2. Spacing and Paragraphs
The entire document must be one-and-a-half spaced. This rule applies uniformly to all sections, including block quotes, extracts, and bibliographic material. Do not use double spacing. When indicating a new paragraph, use the standard tab indentation. Do not insert extra blank lines between paragraphs to indicate a break. If a distinct section break is required within a chapter, denote this by placing three asterisks (***) centered on a dedicated line.

3. Headings and Subheadings
Chapter titles should be centered and formatted in bold. Subheadings within the text should also be bolded, but aligned flush left. The first line of text immediately following a subheading should not be indented.

4. Citations and Footnotes
Lantern Press utilizes a numbered footnote system rather than in-text parenthetical citations. Note numbers within the main text must be formatted as superscript. The corresponding notes must be placed together at the end of the manuscript in a dedicated "Notes" section, organized by chapter. Do not place footnotes at the bottom of individual pages. When referencing book titles within these notes, use italics. For journal articles or individual poems, enclose the title in standard quotation marks. Strict adherence to these guidelines significantly reduces production time and prevents unnecessary editorial revisions.